Bug: a held header that is reverted loses its references

header.h declares shared and a.c includes it. A push holds header.h with changed
content, so a.c is built from that. The next request no longer holds header.h, as
after a revert in the editor, and a push by name on shared answers "Definition not
found". Without the held content in between it goes to header.h:1.

Cause, from the trace: closeEditorBuffersNoLongerHeld() (src/editor.c) closes the
buffer at the start of the request and calls reparseFile(), which for a header only
strips it (removeReferenceableItemsForFile). Nothing builds its includers. a.c's
knowledge is newer than header.h on disk, so it looks current and is not rebuilt.
A push from a.c would hide it, since its cursor parse re-emits the header; a push
by name does not parse.

It breaks ADR-0032's all or nothing: a header is stripped only when every CU that
includes it is built in the same request. Likely fix: a header whose buffer closes
goes to the build list like a changed held header, its includers with it, instead
of a strip of its own. For a CU, closeEditorBuffersNoLongerHeld() also builds right
there, before the scan and outside the build list. Decide first whether closing a
buffer should mark the file for the build list rather than act.

expected has the goto to header.h:1 that the same requests give without the held
content (WSL, session d25255b8, 2026-10-12).
